A.mc("e2-c10-standard-1-check","c10-1-core","What process forms the manganese nodules described in this section?",["*Precipitation of manganese from seawater|The section describes manganese leaving solution and accumulating into nodules.", "Solidification of lava in a narrow fracture|That forms an igneous dike, not these precipitated nodules.", "Compression of a buried shell-rich reef|That does not explain the seawater-derived manganese nodules.", "Melting of sediment inside a drill pipe|The drill retrieves samples; melting is not the nodule mechanism."],"Nodule formation is chemical precipitation; recovery and resource assessment are later investigative steps.",["TB10:10.1"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C10S1"]});
A.mc("e2-c10-standard-1-visual","c10-1-core","In the satellite diagram, why can a submarine mountain be associated with a slightly elevated sea surface?",["*Its gravitational attraction redistributes seawater|The source links the mountain mass to a sea-surface height anomaly.", "The satellite pushes seawater toward the mountain|The instrument measures height; it does not raise the water.", "Sunlight directly outlines the deep mountain|The technique is an indirect height/gravity inference.", "Ocean drilling lifts the entire basin floor|Drilling samples a restricted location and does not produce the anomaly."],"The observation is surface height; broad bottom relief is inferred through gravity.",["TB10:10.1"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C10S1"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "animations/chapter10/source/10.1-p3-Im5.png", "alt": "Supplied section 10.1 course figure, PDF page 3", "cap": "Exploring Geology · 10.1 · PDF p3 · original source figure, unchanged geometry"}], "sourceFigure": true});
