A.mc("e2-c10-standard-6-check","c10-6-core","Which description fits the Gulf of California boundary system in the source?",["*Long transform faults link short spreading centers|The source links Baja displacement to this combined geometry.", "One uninterrupted trench surrounds both coastlines|That does not match the stated transform-and-spreading system.", "A stationary reef creates all of the basin’s crust|Reef growth does not explain the plate boundary.", "A mountain load flexes an entirely unrifted foreland|That is a different basin mechanism."],"Baja displacement and local spreading must both be included.",["TB10:10.6"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C10S6"]});
A.mc("e2-c10-standard-6-visual","c10-6-core","What interpretation follows from the Bering cross section’s active offshore arc and labeled inactive former subduction zone?",["*Oceanic crust became trapped behind a shifted arc system|The source reconstructs a seaward boundary shift leaving older crust behind it.", "Every ocean–continent contact is presently subducting|The inactive former zone contradicts that claim.", "The whole sea must be under one shallow continental shelf|The figure includes a deeper oceanic basin.", "No plate boundary can exist anywhere near this sea|The offshore arc is part of an active system."],"A passive mainland edge can coexist with an active arc farther offshore.",["TB10:10.6"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C10S6"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "animations/chapter10/source/10.6-p3-Im4.png", "alt": "Supplied section 10.6 course figure, PDF page 3", "cap": "Exploring Geology · 10.6 · PDF p3 · original source figure, unchanged geometry"}], "sourceFigure": true});
