A.mc("e2-c10-standard-7-check","c10-7-core","Why is the Mediterranean an unsuitable example for the claim “every part of a sea has one tectonic origin”?",["*Its western and eastern regions record different histories|The source contrasts western rifting with eastern inherited ocean and convergence.", "Its entire floor formed as a coral atoll|That is not the source interpretation.", "No continental crust occurs near the Mediterranean|The sea lies among converging continental regions.", "Its map contains no evidence of changing boundaries|The section explicitly discusses changing tectonic settings."],"Treat the sea as a region with several histories, not one uniform tectonic label.",["TB10:10.7"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C10S7"]});
A.mc("e2-c10-standard-7-visual","c10-7-core","In the Persian Gulf section, what relationship connects the basin with the Zagros Mountains?",["*Thrust loading bends the adjacent Arabian plate downward|The source section places a flexural trough beside the mountain load.", "The basin is the center of a new hot-spot volcano|The shown relation is loading and flexure, not a hot-spot track.", "Salt must have melted the entire continental crust|The geometry does not establish crustal melting.", "Normal faults alone pull the mountains away from Arabia|That does not explain the collisional loading shown."],"The mountain load and adjacent basin are parts of the same flexural system.",["TB10:10.7"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C10S7"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "animations/chapter10/source/10.7-p2-Im4.png", "alt": "Supplied section 10.7 course figure, PDF page 2", "cap": "Exploring Geology · 10.7 · PDF p2 · original source figure, unchanged geometry"}, {"kind": "img", "src": "animations/chapter10/source/10.7-p2-Im5.png", "alt": "Supplied section 10.7 course figure, PDF page 2", "cap": "Exploring Geology · 10.7 · PDF p2 · original source figure, unchanged geometry"}], "sourceFigure": true});
