A.mc("e2-c10-standard-10-check","c10-10-core","How can repeated seawater inflow help build a thick evaporite sequence in a restricted basin?",["*It replenishes water and dissolved components during continued evaporation|Repeated replenishment allows more material to precipitate over time.", "It guarantees that salt can never precipitate|The source allows inflow and deposition to coexist.", "It turns every salt mineral into basalt|There is no such igneous conversion in the process.", "It requires melting all the deposited halite|Salt deposition is precipitation from solution."],"A thick accumulation can reflect prolonged replenishment and concentration, not just one basin volume.",["TB10:10.10"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C10S10"]});
A.mc("e2-c10-standard-10-visual","c10-10-core","What is the source’s interpretation of the unusual bumps and pits in the Gulf Coast seafloor block?",["*Buried salt movement deforms the overlying sedimentary cover|The section connects salt flow with domes, folds, faults and surface relief.", "They all identify newly erupting mid-ocean-ridge volcanoes|The Gulf salt model is not a ridge-eruption diagram.", "They prove that every sediment layer is horizontal at depth|The diagram shows deformation and faulting.", "They require the salt to be a molten mantle plume|The salt deforms as a solid geological material."],"Connect surface relief with the subsurface structures rather than equating every bump with a volcano.",["TB10:10.10"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C10S10"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "animations/chapter10/source/10.10-p5-Im8.png", "alt": "Supplied section 10.10 course figure, PDF page 5", "cap": "Exploring Geology · 10.10 · PDF p5 · original source figure, unchanged geometry"}], "sourceFigure": true});
A.match("e2-c10-standard-10-bank","c10-10-core","Match the complete bank to these source-supported observations or relationships. A bank term may be used more than once when two observations share the same classification.",[["Evaporation raises dissolved-salt concentration", "Brine concentration"], ["Minerals leave solution and form layers", "Evaporite precipitation"], ["Buried weak salt rises through denser cover", "Salt dome"], ["An exposed salt mass spreads downhill", "Salt glacier"], ["Weak salt permits overlying rocks to fold and fault", "Salt-assisted detachment"]],"Precipitating a salt layer and deforming it later are two different geological events.",["TB10:10.10"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C10S10"], "wordBank": true, "sourceFigure": false});
