A.cards("c10s12",[{"id": "g10-12-standard", "h": "10.12 · Read the evidence and complete the explanation", "c": ["c10-12-core"], "src": ["TB10:10.12"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate inherited Gulf structure from active Caribbean boundaries</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Jurassic extension and spreading opened the Gulf, while restricted basins accumulated salt and later sediment buried the older structures. Spreading in the Gulf then ceased in the source reconstruction.</li><li>The Caribbean combines thick basaltic crust, transforms, trenches and arcs. The Cayman Trough and Lesser Antilles are different kinds of boundary feature.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Retain the source’s unresolved statements</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The present-setting text describes Atlantic lithosphere descending westward beneath the Caribbean. A later line calls the Lesser Antilles zone east-dipping. This is an <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">internal source conflict</mark>; practice uses the present map and westward relationship rather than keying contradictory directions.</li><li>The source also discusses debate over a Pacific origin versus local formation of the Caribbean basaltic plateau. A reconstructed path is a model, not a directly observed journey.</li><li>On-land maps and ages, seafloor surveys, seismic sections, drilling and paleomagnetic/spreading reconstructions provide complementary tests.</li></ul></section>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Separate passive-margin history, thick basaltic crust, transforms and subduction."], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "animations/chapter10/source/10.12-p3-Im3.png", "alt": "Exploring Geology · 10.12 · PDF p3 · original course figure", "cap": "Exploring Geology · 10.12 · PDF p3 · original source figure, unchanged geometry"}]}]);
