A.cards("c10s13",[{"id": "g10-13-standard", "h": "10.13 · Read the evidence and complete the explanation", "c": ["c10-13-core"], "src": ["TB10:10.13"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use every numbered location as evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Number 1 is the broad abyssal region; number 2 identifies the basaltic plateau and hot-spot track; number 3 is the volcanic arc beside the trench; number 4 is the back-arc spreading system.</li><li>Number 5 is the quiet western continental edge, even though its shelf is narrow. Number 6 is the broad central margin with canyons and sediment delivery. Number 8 identifies the eastern spreading ridge and number 9 the eastern continental shelf.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">A–A′ section</mark> is a separate cross-sectional line; the source does not include a location marker 7 on this terrain image. Preserve the actual labels instead of inventing a missing number.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct the past and project the future conditionally</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The plateau existed 40 million years ago, but a 19 Ma volcano did not yet exist 20 million years ago. The active southeast end and older northwest chain constrain relative motion.</li><li>Oldest oceanic crust near the eastern margins, around 20–25 Ma, constrains early basin opening. A precise speed additionally needs a distance and an explicit motion model.</li><li>For a future sketch, state that motion continues. Buoyant plateau material approaching subduction may accrete; a future collision is a conditional scenario, not an observed event.</li><li>Assignment 17’s tiny four-alternative image remains inadequate for exact-image grading. The readable textbook <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">A–A′ section</mark> supports the normal-faulted relation without recreating unavailable answer geometry.</li></ul></section>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use numbered locations, age patterns and boundary geometry to reconstruct a changing region."], "media": []}]);
